1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,009
<font color="#000000">This paper asks a fundamental question in learning
theory: how well does plain logistic</font>

2
00:00:06,089 --> 00:00:10,185
<font color="#000000">regression solve the problem of agnostically
learning halfspaces?</font>

3
00:00:10,265 --> 00:00:12,433
<font color="#000000">A halfspace is a linear classifier,</font>

4
00:00:12,513 --> 00:00:17,572
<font color="#000000">and in the agnostic setting an adversary corrupts
an OPT fraction of the labels,</font>

5
00:00:17,652 --> 00:00:21,941
<font color="#000000">so the best any linear classifier can do is a
zero-one error of OPT.</font>

6
00:00:22,021 --> 00:00:27,594
<font color="#000000">Prior work left a wide gap: a lower bound saying no
convex surrogate can beat order OPT,</font>

7
00:00:27,674 --> 00:00:33,119
<font color="#000000">and an upper bound showing logistic regression
achieves only order square-root of OPT.</font>

8
00:00:33,199 --> 00:00:34,853
<font color="#000000">The authors close this gap.</font>

9
00:00:34,933 --> 00:00:40,506
<font color="#000000">They construct a well-behaved distribution on which
the global minimizer of the logistic</font>

10
00:00:40,586 --> 00:00:43,333
<font color="#000000">risk provably suffers square-root OPT error,</font>

11
00:00:43,413 --> 00:00:45,838
<font color="#000000">matching the known upper bound exactly.</font>

12
00:00:45,918 --> 00:00:51,813
<font color="#000000">They then show that adding a natural
radial-Lipschitzness assumption lets logistic regression</font>

13
00:00:51,893 --> 00:00:54,061
<font color="#000000">reach near-optimal order-OPT error.</font>

14
00:00:54,141 --> 00:00:57,851
<font color="#000000">Finally, they give a strikingly simple two-phase
algorithm,</font>

15
00:00:57,931 --> 00:01:01,063
<font color="#000000">logistic regression followed by a perceptron step,</font>

16
00:01:01,143 --> 00:01:05,303
<font color="#000000">that attains near-optimal error for any
well-behaved distribution,</font>

17
00:01:05,383 --> 00:01:09,800
<font color="#000000">replacing the many rounds of optimization that
prior methods required.</font>

18
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:14,790
<font color="#000000">The paper studies the agnostic learning of
homogeneous halfspaces,</font>

19
00:01:14,870 --> 00:01:19,480
<font color="#000000">one of the most fundamental problems in machine learning.</font>

20
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:24,170
<font color="#000000">We are given samples from an unknown distribution
over feature-label pairs,</font>

21
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:28,860
<font color="#000000">and we want a linear classifier whose zero-one
error is close to OPT,</font>

22
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:33,550
<font color="#000000">the best error achievable by any homogeneous halfspace.</font>

23
00:01:33,630 --> 00:01:38,240
<font color="#000000">Equivalently,</font>

24
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:42,930
<font color="#000000">an adversary is allowed to flip an OPT fraction of
the labels of an otherwise perfectly linearly separable dataset.</font>

25
00:01:43,010 --> 00:01:47,620
<font color="#000000">Logistic regression is the natural, ubiquitous
heuristic here,</font>

26
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:52,310
<font color="#000000">yet its theoretical guarantees for this problem
were poorly understood.</font>

27
00:01:52,390 --> 00:01:57,000
<font color="#000000">Prior results left a frustrating gap: a lower bound
saying no convex surrogate loss can beat order OPT error,</font>

28
00:01:57,080 --> 00:02:01,690
<font color="#000000">against an upper bound showing logistic regression
reaches only order square-root of OPT.</font>

29
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:06,380
<font color="#000000">The central question is which of these bounds
reflects the truth for logistic regression.</font>

30
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:11,870
<font color="#000000">Logistic regression is arguably the most widely
deployed classification algorithm,</font>

31
00:02:11,950 --> 00:02:16,110
<font color="#000000">so understanding its statistical guarantees is not
merely academic.</font>

32
00:02:16,190 --> 00:02:20,920
<font color="#000000">The worry is that logistic regression can behave
terribly in the worst case;</font>

33
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,362
<font color="#000000">earlier work showed its risk minimizer can be wrong
on nearly a one-minus-OPT fraction</font>

34
00:02:26,442 --> 00:02:29,590
<font color="#000000">of examples on an adversarially built distribution.</font>

35
00:02:29,670 --> 00:02:34,780
<font color="#000000">To rule out such pathologies, the community focuses
on well-behaved distributions,</font>

36
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:39,843
<font color="#000000">such as isotropic log-concave ones, where much
stronger guarantees are possible.</font>

37
00:02:39,923 --> 00:02:45,602
<font color="#000000">But even under these assumptions the precise error
rate of logistic regression was unknown,</font>

38
00:02:45,682 --> 00:02:49,526
<font color="#000000">sitting somewhere between order OPT and order
square-root OPT.</font>

39
00:02:49,606 --> 00:02:54,968
<font color="#000000">Pinning down this rate tells us whether logistic
regression is a near-optimal agnostic</font>

40
00:02:55,048 --> 00:02:59,082
<font color="#000000">learner or whether it fundamentally leaves accuracy
on the table,</font>

41
00:02:59,162 --> 00:03:02,120
<font color="#000000">and if so, what minimal fix recovers optimality.</font>

42
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:06,551
<font color="#000000">The paper makes three contributions that together
resolve the gap.</font>

43
00:03:06,631 --> 00:03:10,306
<font color="#000000">First, it constructs an explicit two-dimensional,
isotropic,</font>

44
00:03:10,386 --> 00:03:15,626
<font color="#000000">well-behaved distribution on which the global
minimizer of the logistic risk provably</font>

45
00:03:15,706 --> 00:03:18,067
<font color="#000000">attains square-root OPT zero-one error,</font>

46
00:03:18,147 --> 00:03:23,762
<font color="#000000">matching the known upper bound and proving that
logistic regression alone cannot do better.</font>

47
00:03:23,842 --> 00:03:26,954
<font color="#000000">Second, it identifies a clean sufficient condition,</font>

48
00:03:27,034 --> 00:03:29,207
<font color="#000000">radial Lipschitzness of the density,</font>

49
00:03:29,287 --> 00:03:33,901
<font color="#000000">under which logistic regression does reach the
near-optimal order-OPT rate.</font>

50
00:03:33,981 --> 00:03:35,465
<font color="#000000">Third, and most usefully,</font>

51
00:03:35,545 --> 00:03:39,721
<font color="#000000">it introduces a simple two-phase algorithm: run
logistic regression,</font>

52
00:03:39,801 --> 00:03:45,229
<font color="#000000">then run a perceptron-style hinge-loss step from
that warm start on a restricted domain.</font>

53
00:03:45,309 --> 00:03:51,988
<font color="#000000">This attains near-optimal error for every
well-behaved distribution without the radial-Lipschitz assumption,</font>

54
00:03:52,068 --> 00:03:55,430
<font color="#000000">at the cost of just one additional convex optimization,</font>

55
00:03:55,510 --> 00:04:02,440
<font color="#000000">and it is far simpler than prior algorithms that
required solving a logarithmic number of minimization problems.</font>

56
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:06,277
<font color="#000000">The technical core rests on two convex surrogate losses.</font>

57
00:04:06,357 --> 00:04:10,255
<font color="#000000">The logistic loss is the log of one plus e to the
minus margin,</font>

58
00:04:10,335 --> 00:04:14,234
<font color="#000000">and the hinge loss is the positive part of the
negative margin;</font>

59
00:04:14,314 --> 00:04:17,897
<font color="#000000">both are minimized as empirical averages over the samples.</font>

60
00:04:17,977 --> 00:04:19,160
<font color="#000000">For the lower bound,</font>

61
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:23,581
<font color="#000000">the authors hand-design a distribution Q on the
plane with four parts,</font>

62
00:04:23,661 --> 00:04:27,244
<font color="#000000">a construction valid when OPT is at most one over sixteen,</font>

63
00:04:27,324 --> 00:04:32,548
<font color="#000000">engineered so that the true optimal halfspace is
only wrong on a small noisy region,</font>

64
00:04:32,628 --> 00:04:39,180
<font color="#000000">yet the logistic risk minimizer is pulled into a
direction that misclassifies a square-root OPT fraction.</font>

65
00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:41,579
<font color="#000000">For the upper bound and the algorithm,</font>

66
00:04:41,659 --> 00:04:47,200
<font color="#000000">the key object is the angle between the learned
direction and the ground-truth direction.</font>

67
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:54,526
<font color="#000000">Phase one runs projected gradient descent on the
logistic risk inside a ball of radius one over square-root epsilon,</font>

68
00:04:54,606 --> 00:05:00,715
<font color="#000000">which under well-behaved-ness returns a direction
within angle order square-root OPT of the truth.</font>

69
00:05:00,795 --> 00:05:05,515
<font color="#000000">Phase two then runs projected stochastic gradient
descent on the hinge loss,</font>

70
00:05:05,595 --> 00:05:08,609
<font color="#000000">which is exactly the classical perceptron update,</font>

71
00:05:08,689 --> 00:05:12,777
<font color="#000000">starting from that warm start and confined to a
restricted domain.</font>

72
00:05:12,857 --> 00:05:18,840
<font color="#000000">This second convex step sharpens the angle and
drives the zero-one error down to near order OPT.</font>

73
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:21,568
<font color="#000000">Because this is a learning-theory paper,</font>

74
00:05:21,648 --> 00:05:24,724
<font color="#000000">there are no benchmark datasets or empirical tables;</font>

75
00:05:24,804 --> 00:05:30,126
<font color="#000000">the "data" are distribution classes and the results
are theorems with explicit constants.</font>

76
00:05:30,206 --> 00:05:36,135
<font color="#000000">The positive results hold for the standard family
of well-behaved distributions used in prior work,</font>

77
00:05:36,215 --> 00:05:43,055
<font color="#000000">meaning the marginal on features is isotropic and
satisfies soft-margin and sub-exponential regularity conditions.</font>

78
00:05:43,135 --> 00:05:50,156
<font color="#000000">The negative result is carried by a concrete
counterexample: an explicit distribution Q on the two-dimensional plane,</font>

79
00:05:50,236 --> 00:05:54,223
<font color="#000000">built from four parts and illustrated in the
paper's single figure,</font>

80
00:05:54,303 --> 00:05:57,804
<font color="#000000">that is fully well-behaved yet defeats logistic
regression.</font>

81
00:05:57,884 --> 00:06:00,414
<font color="#000000">The upper bounds are stated in two regimes,</font>

82
00:06:00,494 --> 00:06:04,420
<font color="#000000">one for bounded feature distributions where the
norm is at most B,</font>

83
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:07,455
<font color="#000000">and one for sub-exponential feature distributions,</font>

84
00:06:07,535 --> 00:06:12,250
<font color="#000000">so the guarantees span the settings that matter for
isotropic log-concave data.</font>

85
00:06:12,630 --> 00:06:15,852
<font color="#000000">The headline finding is a matching pair of bounds.</font>

86
00:06:15,932 --> 00:06:18,824
<font color="#000000">On the constructed well-behaved distribution,</font>

87
00:06:18,904 --> 00:06:26,552
<font color="#000000">the global minimizer of the population logistic
risk has zero-one error at least square-root OPT divided by sixty pi,</font>

88
00:06:26,632 --> 00:06:32,694
<font color="#000000">which asymptotically matches the best known upper
bound and therefore proves that square-root</font>

89
00:06:32,774 --> 00:06:36,062
<font color="#000000">OPT is the true rate for logistic regression alone.</font>

90
00:06:36,142 --> 00:06:40,422
<font color="#000000">That is the sense in which the paper closes the
long-standing gap.</font>

91
00:06:40,502 --> 00:06:43,856
<font color="#000000">Then, by adding radial Lipschitzness of the density,</font>

92
00:06:43,936 --> 00:06:48,413
<font color="#000000">the same logistic minimizer jumps to near-optimal
error of order OPT,</font>

93
00:06:48,493 --> 00:06:53,036
<font color="#000000">up to a constant Cκ that is bounded whenever the
density is Lipschitz.</font>

94
00:06:53,116 --> 00:06:55,150
<font color="#000000">And without any such assumption,</font>

95
00:06:55,230 --> 00:07:00,896
<font color="#000000">the two-phase algorithm achieves order OPT plus
epsilon error for bounded distributions</font>

96
00:07:00,976 --> 00:07:04,991
<font color="#000000">and order OPT times log one over OPT for
sub-exponential ones,</font>

97
00:07:05,071 --> 00:07:06,840
<font color="#000000">in expectation over the run.</font>

98
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:11,025
<font color="#000000">Although this is a theory paper without
experimental ablations,</font>

99
00:07:11,105 --> 00:07:15,711
<font color="#000000">the analysis is structured to expose which
assumptions buy which guarantees,</font>

100
00:07:15,791 --> 00:07:17,869
<font color="#000000">playing the role an ablation would.</font>

101
00:07:17,949 --> 00:07:23,172
<font color="#000000">The single upper-bound argument is versatile enough
to recover the earlier square-root</font>

102
00:07:23,252 --> 00:07:27,242
<font color="#000000">OPT rate for general well-behaved distributions as
a special case,</font>

103
00:07:27,322 --> 00:07:32,298
<font color="#000000">and crucially it also goes through for the hinge
loss, not just the logistic loss;</font>

104
00:07:32,378 --> 00:07:36,492
<font color="#000000">this dual applicability is what enables the
perceptron second phase.</font>

105
00:07:36,572 --> 00:07:40,808
<font color="#000000">The theorem cleanly separates the bounded and
sub-exponential regimes,</font>

106
00:07:40,888 --> 00:07:46,111
<font color="#000000">showing that the only price of moving from bounded
to heavier sub-exponential tails is</font>

107
00:07:46,191 --> 00:07:48,824
<font color="#000000">an extra logarithmic factor in one over OPT.</font>

108
00:07:48,904 --> 00:07:53,449
<font color="#000000">Likewise, the constant Cκ, an integral of the
radial-Lipschitzness modulus,</font>

109
00:07:53,529 --> 00:07:57,519
<font color="#000000">isolates exactly how much the density's radial
smoothness matters,</font>

110
00:07:57,599 --> 00:08:00,540
<font color="#000000">vanishing when the density is radially symmetric.</font>

111
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:05,396
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

112
00:08:05,476 --> 00:08:09,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lower bound gives a zero-one error of at least
square-root OPT over sixty pi for the logistic minimizer,</font>

113
00:08:10,032 --> 00:08:14,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">valid whenever OPT is at most one one-hundredth.</font>

114
00:08:14,587 --> 00:08:19,063
<font color="#FFFFFF">Under radial Lipschitzness, logistic regression
reaches near-optimal order-OPT error.</font>

115
00:08:19,143 --> 00:08:23,619
<font color="#FFFFFF">The two-phase algorithm achieves order OPT plus
epsilon error for bounded distributions</font>

116
00:08:23,699 --> 00:08:28,175
<font color="#FFFFFF">and order OPT times log one over OPT for
sub-exponential ones.</font>

117
00:08:28,255 --> 00:08:32,731
<font color="#FFFFFF">It does so with a sample complexity of order d over
epsilon squared,</font>

118
00:08:32,811 --> 00:08:37,287
<font color="#FFFFFF">a quadratic improvement over the d over epsilon to
the fourth required by prior nonconvex methods.</font>

119
00:08:37,367 --> 00:08:41,842
<font color="#FFFFFF">And structurally,</font>

120
00:08:41,923 --> 00:08:46,398
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas earlier algorithms that hit order OPT error
had to solve a logarithmic number</font>

121
00:08:46,478 --> 00:08:50,954
<font color="#FFFFFF">of minimization problems while guessing OPT by
binary search,</font>

122
00:08:51,034 --> 00:08:55,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">this method needs only two convex steps.</font>

123
00:08:55,890 --> 00:08:59,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message is twofold.</font>

124
00:09:00,042 --> 00:09:04,115
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

125
00:09:04,195 --> 00:09:08,267
<font color="#FFFFFF">logistic regression by itself cannot beat
square-root OPT error for agnostically learning halfspaces,</font>

126
00:09:08,347 --> 00:09:12,419
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the paper proves this rate is exactly tight by
constructing a distribution that forces it.</font>

127
00:09:12,499 --> 00:09:16,572
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

128
00:09:16,652 --> 00:09:20,724
<font color="#FFFFFF">the fix is remarkably cheap: appending one
perceptron-style convex minimization,</font>

129
00:09:20,804 --> 00:09:24,876
<font color="#FFFFFF">warm-started from the logistic solution and
confined to a bounded domain,</font>

130
00:09:24,956 --> 00:09:29,028
<font color="#FFFFFF">provably boosts the guarantee to near-optimal
order-OPT error for every well-behaved distribution.</font>

131
00:09:29,108 --> 00:09:33,181
<font color="#FFFFFF">Along the way the paper also identifies radial
Lipschitzness of the feature density as</font>

132
00:09:33,261 --> 00:09:37,333
<font color="#FFFFFF">the natural condition under which logistic
regression alone already succeeds.</font>

133
00:09:37,413 --> 00:09:41,485
<font color="#FFFFFF">In short, a passerby can remember that logistic
regression is half the answer,</font>

134
00:09:41,565 --> 00:09:45,638
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a single extra convex step completes it,</font>

135
00:09:45,718 --> 00:09:49,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">more simply and with better sample complexity than
any prior method.</font>
